<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns="http://www.semanticweb.org/vicki/ontologies/2026/5/untitled-ontology-28/"
     xml:base="http://www.semanticweb.org/vicki/ontologies/2026/5/untitled-ontology-28/"
     xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
     xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
     xmlns:www="http://www.cair.za.net/"
     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:cair="http://cair.za.net/"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:vann="http://purl.org/vocab/vann/"
     xmlns:terms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
     xmlns:schema="https://schema.org/">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://w3id.org/defeasible/acp-defonto">
        <cair:consistent rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#boolean">true</cair:consistent>
        <terms:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0003-3641-4849</terms:contributor>
        <terms:created>2026-11-15</terms:created>
        <terms:creator>Victoria Chama</terms:creator>
        <terms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0"/>
        <terms:rights>This ontology is openly available under CC BY 4.0.</terms:rights>
        <terms:rights>public</terms:rights>
        <terms:title>ACP-DefOnto</terms:title>
        <terms:title xml:lang="en">Access Control and Policy Defeasible Ontology (ACP-DefOnto)</terms:title>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">This ontology provides a formal representation of access control and policy mechanisms within organizational, governmental, and computational domains. It captures entities such as agents, employees, systems, and resources, along with the permissions, prohibitions, authorizations, and contextual constraints that regulate access.

The ontology is designed within a defeasible reasoning framework, acknowledging that access control policies are rarely absolute. Rules typically confer rights or impose restrictions, yet they may be subject to exceptions, priority relations, and overriding conditions. For example, general access permissions can be suspended by judicial injunctions, presidential waivers, or emergency overrides.

By encoding both default rules and their exceptions, the ontology supports non-monotonic inference over access control scenarios. This enables reasoning not only about what is normally permissible, but also about cases where higher-priority rules or situational contexts alter default outcomes. Such modeling enhances the expressive power of access control ontologies, allowing for a more realistic treatment of complex policy domains.</rdfs:comment>
        <owl:versionInfo rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1.0</owl:versionInfo>
        <schema:description>The Access Control and Policy ontology represents knowledge relating to access permissions, users, roles, resources, organisational policies, and contextual access restrictions.

Access control is a particularly relevant domain for defeasible reasoning because access decisions frequently depend on general policies that admit exceptions.</schema:description>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


